{"author":{"uri":{"label":"https://itunes.apple.com/gb/reviews/id163382189"}, "name":{"label":"447980981475"}, "label":""}, "updated":{"label":"2025-09-24T02:03:34-07:00"}, "im:rating":{"label":"5"}, "im:version":{"label":"1.14.1"}, "id":{"label":"13177753546"}, "title":{"label":"Simple to use, pretty, and the data is yours"}, "content":{"label":"I've previously used – or in fact not used – another, much more comprehensive, app.  I switched to HabitKit as a fresh start.  Here's why it's great:\n\nIt's simple, without masses of bells and whistles.  That means I actually get stuff done rather than fiddling with it endlessly.  I hope it always stays simple.\n\nIt's really pretty.  You *want* to use it.  That's a huge advantage!\n\nIt's not based around some opaque cloudy AI nonsense: your data is yours and you can export it in comprehensible JSON which you could easily massage in other programs and then import again.  That's a huge win for me.\n\nIt has the usual subscription thing.  But there's a lifetime option: work out how many hours you need to get back to pay for that and it is not many.\n\nIt's great.\n\nHere are three improvements I would like:\n\nMulti-step habits should have options to name the step (so 'minutes doing x' say) and should be weighted so completing, say, 60 minutes counts as one completion (I've asked the author about this: he's very responsive).\n\nIt would be nice if it had a feature to automagically dump the JSON data to a file once a day or so.\n\nIt would be nice if the JSON format was documented (it might be, I haven't checked this in fact).", "attributes":{"type":"text"}}, "link":{"attributes":{"rel":"related", "href":"https://itunes.apple.com/gb/review?id=6443918070&type=Purple%20Software"}}, "im:voteSum":{"label":"0"}, "im:contentType":{"attributes":{"term":"Application", "label":"Application"}}, "im:voteCount":{"label":"0"}}, 
